$DIR/main.p:9:3: cannot pass &v to put, which keeps it after this iteration: the loop at line 7 reuses v in every iteration
    &v is a pointer to v. v lives in main's frame, where every iteration of the
    loop at line 7 reuses the same memory for it. put stores it in buf[i]
    (main.p:3). When the next iteration starts, what put kept would see that
    iteration's v, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    If put needs the view only during the call, change it so it does not keep
    it. To use the value later, keep the value instead of a view of it:
        var saved int // declared before the loop
        ...
        saved = v
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
